        "id": "p19-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many three-digit numbers have no two digits that are the same?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$648$",
          "worked": "The first digit has 9 choices because it cannot be zero. The second digit has 9 choices, including zero but excluding the first digit. The third digit has 8 choices.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=9\\times9\\times8\\\\N&=648\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "How many four-digit numbers have no two digits that are the same?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4536$",
          "worked": "The first digit has 9 choices because it cannot be zero. The remaining digits have 9, 8 and 7 choices respectively, since zero is allowed and previously chosen digits are excluded.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=9\\times9\\times8\\times7\\\\N&=4536\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
